Subregion name: 'base_south'
Subregion description: Southern team spawn base: a fortified staging compound with a protected spawn area, a forward exit gate/balcony feeding into the lanes, and an objective capture/plant site just in front of it. Weathered corrugated-metal and concrete refinery architecture.
Subregion plan: This region is the southern team's fortified staging base, occupying the full 60 m width of the map along its southern edge and 12 m of depth, backing against the southern perimeter wall. It is organized in three depth bands running from the rear wall outward toward the lanes. The rearmost band, against the southern wall, is the protected spawn area: a sheltered, roofed or partially enclosed compound of weathered corrugated-metal and concrete refinery structures that shields players at the moment they enter the playspace and blocks sightlines and fire from the lanes, with multiple internal walkways feeding forward. The middle band is the forward exit zone: a fortified gate and balcony structure with controlled openings and a raised platform giving an elevated firing position and sightline outward, splitting players into routes that align with the three lanes ahead — the western opening feeding toward left_lane, the central opening toward central_lane, and the eastern opening toward right_lane. The frontmost band, at the northern edge of the region just before the lanes begin, is a more open courtyard holding the objective capture/plant site, ringed with low cover and tactical clutter so it can be attacked and defended. Verticality is layered: ground-level cover and barriers below, the elevated exit balcony and connecting ramps or stairs above, plus scaffolding and gantries integrated into the compound to provide multiple peek heights toward the central lane. All architecture uses the unified abandoned-refinery vocabulary: corrugated steel sheeting, concrete bases, riveted steel framing, rust streaks, fuel-tank and machinery forms, and faded faction stencils. The overall footprint must be left-right mirror-symmetric about the map's central axis and is the reflected counterpart of base_north.

